@namespace DotNetNuke.Common.Controls
@inherits DotNetNuke.Entities.Modules.PortalModuleBase

@using System
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components

@*
    Replaces message.ascx + Message.ascx.cs + Message.ascx.designer.cs.

    The control is a message surface: the .ascx was a <div> around
    <asp:Label ID="lblMessage" CssClass="dnnFormMessage dnnFormValidationSummary" />, and the
    code-behind did exactly one thing - `this.ID = "Message"` in InitializeComponent, which is what
    pointed Control.ID (and therefore the WebForms localization walk) at the name "Message". The
    component type is named Message, so that identity is preserved without an assignment.

    Nothing in the solution assigned lblMessage.Text through a designer field; the text was supplied
    by whatever loaded the control. That seam is kept as the Text parameter, so a loader can still
    set it (DnnComponentParameters.Capture carries [Parameter] values through the module pipeline).
    The label rendered its Text as raw markup, so Text is rendered as a MarkupString - a validation
    summary legitimately contains markup, and encoding it would be a behaviour change.

    The file is named Message.razor because a component file may not start with a lowercase letter
    (RZ10011); the TYPE name and namespace are unchanged (DotNetNuke.Common.Controls.Message).
*@

<div><span id="lblMessage" class="dnnFormMessage dnnFormValidationSummary">@((MarkupString)(this.Text ?? string.Empty))</span></div>

@code {
    /// <summary>Gets or sets the message text. Replaces the <c>lblMessage.Text</c> designer field.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string Text { get; set; }
}
